    "teach_3": {
      "id": "teach_3",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "What if the present age is unknown? Give it a name.\n\nSuppose a person is p years old today. Six years ago, the person was six years younger:\n\nPast age = p − 6",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_3_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_3_step": {
      "id": "teach_3_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "The letter p still means today's age. It does not change when the date changes; we write a new expression for the other date.\n\nIf p happens to be 18, then p − 6 = 12. That check shows why we subtract 6 rather than divide by 6.\n\nPast ages must be possible: p = 4 would give −2 six years ago, so that value cannot fit this story.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_3"
        }
      ]
    },
